e('how nested page tables remove the cost of shadow paging','how nested page tables avoid much shadow-paging synchronisation work','EPT not removal of all translation cost.',[m])
e('The guest kernel now runs in **ring 0 of non-root mode**. `POPF` behaves exactly as it would on bare metal because the guest really is in ring 0. The problem of sensitive-but-unprivileged instructions disappears.','On Intel, a conventional guest kernel runs in **ring 0 of VMX non-root operation**. Guest privilege and virtualisation controls let the VMM preserve the intended guest semantics without classical ring compression; VM exits may be architecturally required or selected by controls. AMD uses its own host/guest terminology.', 'Not all exits optional; avoid implying Intel mode names apply identically to AMD.',[sdm])
e('- **Guest state**: registers, control registers, segment state, saved on exit and loaded on entry.','- **Guest state**: architecturally specified control, segment and execution state. The VMCS does not automatically preserve every general-purpose register; VMM software handles additional state.','VMCS is not complete general register save area.',[sdm])
e('- **Host state**: where to jump on exit.','- **Host state**: Intel VMCS fields describe the resume context. AMD VMCB and host-save mechanisms differ; the structures are not identical layouts.','Avoid treating VMCB as same host-state structure.',[sdm])
e('On Linux, the **KVM** kernel module drives VT-x/AMD-V,','On x86 Linux, **KVM** drives VT-x/AMD-V,','KVM supports other architectures.',[k])
e('  ioctl(vcpu_fd, KVM_RUN, 0);','  if (ioctl(vcpu_fd, KVM_RUN, 0) < 0) {\n    if (errno == EINTR) continue;\n    fail("KVM_RUN"); /* exits process */\n  }','Check ioctl errors before using potentially stale exit_reason.',[k])
e('  case KVM_EXIT_HLT:\n    return;\n  }','  case KVM_EXIT_HLT:\n    return; /* toy: stop on guest HLT */\n  default:\n    fail("unhandled KVM exit");\n  }','Explicitly toy halt policy and fail unsupported exits rather than spin.',[k])
e('Many exits (CPUID, CR accesses, most page faults) are handled inside the KVM kernel module without ever returning to userspace. Only device emulation bubbles up to the `switch`. Fewer and cheaper exits mean faster VMs, and much of the last 20 years of hypervisor engineering is about removing them.','This is a control-flow fragment, not a complete VMM: initialise and map the KVM run structure, provide handlers and error support, and define a real halt/wakeup policy. Many hardware exits are handled in KVM without userspace return. Userspace exits also include shutdown, internal errors and other events, not only device emulation. Reducing unnecessary transitions can reduce overhead.','Other KVM userspace exits and initialization requirements omitted.',[k])
e('To keep them in sync, the hypervisor **write-protects** the guest\'s own page tables. Every time the guest edits a page-table entry, it faults, exits, and the hypervisor updates the shadow copy.','One synchronisation strategy **write-protects** guest page-table pages and intercepts writes. Implementations can also use batching, unsynchronised pages and other optimisations; not every write must cause a separate exit.','Shadow paging implementations avoid many per-write exits.',[m])
e('The guest edits its own page tables freely, with no exits. The hypervisor only sets up the GPA→HPA tables, and gets an **EPT violation** exit when the guest touches guest-physical memory that is not mapped yet.','Nested paging normally lets guest page-table writes proceed without shadow-table synchronisation exits. EPT violations can arise from missing mappings or read/write/execute permission restrictions, including monitoring such as dirty logging; they are not limited to first allocation. Other exit reasons remain.','EPT protection failures and monitoring invalidate noexits claim.',[m,sdm])
e('a worst-case walk touches (n + 1)(m + 1) − 1 entries.','an idealised uncached walk with ordinary pages reads (n + 1)(m + 1) − 1 page-table entries, excluding the final data access and accessed/dirty-bit update traffic.','This is structural read count, not bound on all physical memory transactions.',[sdm])
e('With 2 MB pages in the EPT (3 levels),','With 2 MiB leaves in a four-level EPT (3 entries traversed),','Correct binary units and distinction treelevel vs shortened walk.',[sdm])
e('with huge pages in both guest and host, it falls further and the TLB covers 512 times more memory per entry. Memory-heavy databases in VMs gain noticeably from huge pages.','with compatible 2 MiB mappings in both stages, it falls further. A 2 MiB translation covers 512 times the bytes of a 4 KiB translation, but TLB entry counts and achieved performance differ by page size and CPU.','No universal TLB capacity or database speedup.',[sdm])
e('CPU and memory are close to native now. I/O is where VMs still pay. There are three levels.','CPU, memory and I/O costs depend on workload and configuration. Three common I/O approaches illustrate different tradeoffs.','Remove unsupported blanket near-native performance.',[v,m])
e('The guest\'s unmodified e1000 driver writes to device registers; each register write is an MMIO access, an exit, and a trip to userspace. Sending one packet can take several exits. It is compatible but slow.','The guest driver accesses an emulated hardware interface. Trapped register accesses can require device-model work and userspace transitions; batching and acceleration affect the exact count. Emulation provides compatibility but can add overhead.','Avoid everyregisterwriteMMIO/exit/userspace guarantee.',[k])
e('"kick" (1 exit)','notify if needed','Notification suppression/transport mean not exactly oneexit.',[v])
e('virtio-net, virtio-blk, virtio-scsi, virtio-fs and virtio-vsock are standard in KVM, Firecracker and most clouds.','Supported device types depend on the VMM: do not assume that KVM itself, Firecracker and every cloud expose the same virtio devices.','Firecracker does not supply universalvirtio-scsi/fs list.',['https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker/blob/main/docs/design.md'])
e('The fastest option is to give the guest a real device.','Direct assignment gives the guest access to a hardware function and can reduce emulation overhead.','No universalfastest ranking.',[vf])
e('so the device can only write into that guest\'s memory. Data path exits vanish.','so properly configured device DMA is limited to the assigned mappings. IOMMU groups, device topology and interrupt isolation also matter. Normal data transfers can avoid emulation exits; control and interrupt work do not universally disappear.','IOMMU notsole isolation condition orzeroexit guarantee.',[vf])
e('The NIC itself switches packets between VFs. AWS\'s Elastic Network Adapter, Azure Accelerated Networking and most high-performance cloud networking use SR-IOV or a similar hardware model. AWS\'s **Nitro** system pushes this further, moving networking, storage and management onto dedicated cards so the host CPU is nearly all available to guests, with a slim KVM-based hypervisor.','NIC switching and VF-to-VF connectivity depend on hardware and configuration. Cloud platforms can offload networking and storage to dedicated hardware; AWS describes **Nitro** as such an architecture with a lightweight KVM-based hypervisor. This does not establish a universal implementation or available-CPU percentage for cloud services.','Remove unverified everyNICswitch andmostcloud assertions.',['https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/logical-separation/host-and-instance-features.html'])
e('| Emulation | Stock | Slow | Easy |\n| virtio | virtio | Good | Easy |\n| SR-IOV | Vendor VF | Near native | Hard |','| Emulation | Stock | Device-model overhead | Device support needed |\n| virtio | virtio | Batching/acceleration | Backend support needed |\n| SR-IOV | Vendor VF | Direct-assignment path | Hardware support/failover |','Replace categoricalperformance/migration ease.',[vf,v,mig])
e('2. Copy the dirty pages again. Repeat; each round is shorter.','2. Copy dirty pages again. Rounds shrink only when the effective dirty set and transfer capacity permit convergence.','Precopy notalwaysconverges.',[cl])
e('The pause in step 3 is the **downtime**, typically tens to hundreds of milliseconds.','The pause in step 3 is the **downtime**. Its duration depends on remaining state, device handling, transfer rate and orchestration; no universal downtime range is asserted here.','Remove unsourced typical range.',[mig])
e('> A 16 GB VM moves over a link carrying 1.25 GB/s (10 Gbit/s), dirtying 200 MB/s.','> In a decimal-unit toy model, a 16 GB VM has an effective 1.25 GB/s copy rate and 200 MB/s of distinct newly dirty page data. Assume constant rates and no protocol/state-transfer overhead; a nominal 10 Gbit/s link does not guarantee that effective rate.','Distinguish applicationbandwidth vsline speed, uniquedirtypages vswrites.',[cl])
e('> - Round 4: 66 MB takes 52 ms. Stop here and pause.','> - Stop before round 4 and copy the remaining roughly 66 MB while paused: about 52 ms of memory-transfer time, plus CPU/device and switchover work.','Original pausesaftercopyinglastset whilegueststillruns, undercountsnewdirtyset.',[cl])
e('> Each round shrinks by dirty rate ÷ bandwidth = 0.16. If the guest dirties memory **faster** than the link copies it, the ratio is at least 1 and pre-copy never converges.','> In this model each round shrinks by 0.16. If distinct dirty-page production stays at least as fast as effective copying, the set need not shrink. Raw write bandwidth alone is insufficient: repeatedly writing an already-dirty page does not add another page to the set.','Real migration boundedworking set/duplicatewrites invalidates rawrate iff.',[cl])
e('Downtime is tiny, but if the source dies mid-migration the VM is lost, because its memory is split across two hosts.','This can shorten the stop phase, but missing-page faults stall execution. If indispensable pages exist only on the source when it fails, the destination cannot reconstruct them.','Postcopy notuniversal tinydowntime orrobustrecovery.',[pc])
e('the VF\'s state lives inside the NIC hardware, which the hypervisor cannot copy.','VF state lives in hardware and requires explicit device/driver migration support to export and restore.','Some VFdevices supportmigration.',[mig,vf])
e('works on KVM and most clouds, but each level multiplies exits. Useful for CI and labs, rarely for production.','is supported by some KVM configurations and cloud instance types. Exposed CPU features and provider restrictions matter; extra translation and exit handling can add cost, but there is no fixed per-level multiplier.','Remove unsupportedmostcloud/rarelyproductiongeneralisation.',['https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/amazon-ec2-nested-virtualization.html'])
e('- Nested page tables (EPT/NPT) remove shadow paging exits, at the cost of up to 24 memory references per TLB miss; huge pages and page-walk caches mitigate it.','- EPT/NPT avoids much shadow synchronisation work. Four-level tables in both stages need 24 uncached page-table entry reads in the stated structural model; this excludes data and update traffic.','Notabsolute maximumallTLBmissarchitectures.',[sdm,m])
e('- Pre-copy live migration converges only if the dirty rate is below the copy bandwidth; auto-converge and post-copy handle the rest.','- Pre-copy convergence depends on distinct dirty-page production versus effective copying; throttling, post-copy or a longer stop phase have different costs and failure modes.','Avoid iff rawwritebandwidth andguaranteedfallbacksuccess.',[cl,pc])
e('## Further reading','> [!note] Execution and performance gaps\n> The KVM loop is a source-reviewed teaching fragment, not a complete executed VMM. No live migration, VF assignment or device-throughput benchmark was run for this lesson. Exact downtime, exit counts and device performance therefore remain deployment-dependent; unsupported universal figures are omitted.\n\n## Further reading','Visible limitations requiredbyuser.',[k])
